<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/morphoeval-murray-vascular-cube-law-branching> a pk:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Murray (1926a, 1926b), Zamir (1976) & Sherman (1981) minimum-work vascular cube law & arterial branching angles (Homo sapiens (human retinal arterial and microvascular bifurcation network))" ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/murray-vascular-cube-law-branching-1>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/murray-vascular-cube-law-branching-2>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/murray-vascular-cube-law-branching-3>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/murray-vascular-cube-law-branching-4>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/murray-vascular-cube-law-branching-5> ;
    schema:about "Homo sapiens (human retinal arterial and microvascular bifurcation network)" ;
    schema:additionalProperty "does_not_claim: Independent human morphometric confirmation of the F-L exponent shift, of the AVR (an input), or of arteriovenous interdigitation (index 0.504 ~ random mixing, reference 1.0 is an idealisation); verification of the three Pass 3 DOIs (recalled offline, flagged in bib, JSON and script header)",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Fahraeus-Lindqvist eta(r) in the Poiseuille term, generalised stationarity 2 P_met = (4 - eps) P_visc, local gamma_eff(r), symmetric junction exponent gamma_j(r_0) and a Newtonian-vs-F-L tree exponent sweep (gamma* 3.000 -> 2.773)",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Geodesic arterial and venous Murray trees on a 12 mm sphere with tangent-plane Murray angles, AVR-coupled venous root, repulsive A/V coupling with an uncoupled control, orthographic fundus-projection foreshortening and projected A/V crossings",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Green-channel top-hat / Zhang-Suen skeleton / EDT segmentation of the vendored human fundus specimen (N = 19 Y-junctions)",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Power functional, stationarity radius, wall shear invariance, asymmetric cosine laws, Zamir/Steiner/Da Vinci ablation controls, 7-generation Sherman hierarchy and the gamma power sweep (Newtonian)",
        "reported_in_source: Fåhraeus & Lindqvist (1931) / Pries et al. (1992): the apparent viscosity of blood falls in tubes narrower than ~300 um, fitted by eta_rel(D) = 220 e^{-1.3D} + 3.2 - 2.44 e^{-0.06 D^0.645} at H_D = 0.45",
        "reported_in_source: Hubbard et al. (1999): the clinical retinal arteriole-to-venule caliber ratio is about 2/3",
        "reported_in_source: Murray (1926a): minimising Poiseuille pumping power plus a metabolic cost proportional to blood volume gives Q proportional to r^3 and a scale-invariant wall shear stress sqrt(eta b); at the optimum the metabolic term is twice the viscous term",
        "reported_in_source: Murray (1926b): the same functional fixes the bifurcation angles via cos(theta_1) = (r_0^4 + r_1^4 - r_2^4)/(2 r_0^2 r_1^2) and its mirror; symmetric total angle 74.93 deg",
        "reported_in_source: Zamir (1976): alternative optimality principles (surface, volume, drag, power) give different angle laws; Sherman (1981): the cube law holds across generations of a tree",
        "transfer_boundary: The 3D retinal cup is a reduced geometric model: no haemodynamic network solve, no capillary bed connecting arterial and venous tips, no oxygen/VEGF-guided angiogenesis, optic disc at the posterior pole; the venous root radius is an input (AVR = 0.67), not emergent",
        "transfer_boundary: The F-L fit is the in-vitro glass-tube relation at fixed H_D = 0.45 (no in-vivo endothelial surface layer correction, no phase separation at bifurcations), applied per generation to a symmetric tree; the '~2.7' target is reached by the tree optimum (2.773) but not by the local junction exponent (2.859 at 25 um)",
        "transfer_boundary: The J01-J08 'Zamir 1979' junction table carries a Murray-prediction column and agrees with Murray to <= 0.3 deg without page/figure provenance, so the RMSE = 0.134 deg / r = 0.9999 figures are a parity check, not independent validation; the Sherman (1981) hierarchy table is generated from r_g = 82 um * 2^(-g/3), not digitised" ;
